    "stem": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "The fruit fly, <i>Drosophila</i> <i>melanogaster</i>, feeds on sugars found in damaged fruits.",
        "source": {
          "qp": 12
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "A fruit fly with normal features is described as wild type. It has a grey body and its wings are longer than its abdomen. The genes for body colour and wing length are located on different chromosomes.",
        "source": {
          "qp": 12
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "A fruit fly with mutations in these two genes has a black body and short wings.",
        "source": {
          "qp": 12
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Fig. 5.1 shows a wild type fruit fly and a mutant fruit fly.",
        "source": {
          "qp": 12
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "figure",
        "id": "fig5.1",
        "caption": "Fig. 5.1",
        "src": "figures/q5_fig5_1.png",
        "alt": "Wild type and mutant fruit flies",
        "source": {
          "qp": 12
        }
      }
    ],
    "parts": [
      {
        "key": "a",
        "label": "(a)",
        "source": {
          "qp": 12,
          "ms": 11
        },
        "stem": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Fruit flies were first used for genetic crosses by Thomas Morgan in 1908. They are one of the most studied animals in current genetic research.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 12
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "list",
            "items": [
              "Male fruit flies are easily distinguished from female fruit flies.",
              "Fruit flies have a short life cycle and a female can lay hundreds of eggs in a few days.",
              "Some genes for development and cell signalling in fruit flies are similar to those of humans."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Suggest why fruit flies are still used in genetic crosses.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 12
            }
          }
        ],
        "marks": 3,
        "topics": [
          {
            "ref": "16.2(2)",
            "role": "primary"
          }
        ],
        "markScheme": {
          "marks": 3,
          "instruction": "<i>any </i><b><i>three</i></b><i> from: </i>",
          "guidance": [],
          "points": [
            {
              "n": 1,
              "text": "<b>1 </b> small so can keep, in small space / for low cost / with low maintenance <b>; </b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 2,
              "text": "<b>2</b>  can select parents for, mating / cross-breeding / particular crosses <b>; </b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 3,
              "text": "<b>3 </b> so get many, offspring / generations, in a short time <b>;</b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 4,
              "text": "<b>4 </b> can be used to research, cancer / (named) neurodegenerative diseases <b>;</b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 5,
              "text": "<b>5 </b> AVP <b>;</b>  <i> </i>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            }
          ]
        },
        "er": [],
